commit 3031a3067b3a3c5b7a8ca82e995fd3e8e87e39db
Author:     indigoidk <indigoidk@users.noreply.github.com>
AuthorDate: Tue Sep 8 08:38:00 2026 -0500
Commit:     indigoidk <indigoidk@users.noreply.github.com>
CommitDate: Tue Sep 8 08:38:00 2026 -0500

    #514 alphaswpctxreg: the context-switch primitive returned its result in the
    register the ABI tells the caller to discard -- one identifier, rung-3 witnessed
    
    cpu_alpha_palcode.c:250-251 read:
    
        /*  Return old context in v0.  */
        cpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_A0] = cpu->cd.alpha.ctx;
    
    The comment says v0 and the code writes a0.  The third-party ABI wrapper settles
    the intent independently: alpha_pal_swpctx declares `v0 __asm("$0")' an OUTPUT and
    lists a0 among the clobbers.  So the model published into the register the caller is
    told to throw away and left untouched the one it reads.
    
    THE WITNESS IS RUNG 3 AND IT WENT RED FIRST.  regress/alpha_swpctx_v0_probe.py, on
    unmodified -E alpha -e alphabook1 with real call_pal 0x30 instructions:
    
        PREFIX_20260908.out    2/4    measured on fc687db, file UNMODIFIED
        POSTFIX_20260908.out   4/4    same script, after the one-identifier change
    
    THE ORACLE CARRIES THREE DISTINGUISHABLE VALUES, which is what makes it more than a
    value check: an old context C, a new context N, and a sentinel S seeded into v0
    before the second call.  v0 == C is correct; v0 == S is the shipped defect and is
    what HEAD measured; v0 == N would be an INCORRECT repair returning the new context
    instead of the old.  A row asserting only "v0 changed" would pass that third case.
    The design is codex's, from the pass-1 brief's Q4.
    
    AND THE ATTRIBUTION CONTROL IS WHAT MAKES IT A WITNESS RATHER THAN A READING.  The
    `a0 land' row reads a0 after the second call: pre-fix it holds C, proving THE
    HANDLER RAN AND WROTE THE WRONG REGISTER, where a handler that never executed would
    have left a0 holding N.  One observation, two hypotheses separated, and both rows
    flipped together.
    
    No a0 write is added.  The architecture marks a0 UNPREDICTABLE and that definition
    explicitly permits a register to remain unchanged.  An earlier draft of mine called
    a0 "unobservable by construction"; codex refused that as too strong -- software CAN
    read a0, it simply cannot require a value.
    
    PASS 1: SIX SEATS, UNANIMOUS on the fix, the severity and the scope -- codex, grok,
    glm, kimi, deepseek, minimax.  opus5 and fable5 are HELD for the 2026-09-09
    adjudication and agy is the measurement reserve; all three are recorded BY NAME as
    deliberate non-firings, which is neither a seat failure nor agreement.
    
    *** THE PANEL CAUGHT A PREMISE I ASSERTED AND NEVER EXHIBITED, AND THREE SEATS FOUND
    IT INDEPENDENTLY. ***  My brief said "ordering in the handler is correct" while
    showing only :251 and :255.  glm, kimi and minimax all refused to grant it, and kimi
    named the fork exactly: if :255 read the REGISTER rather than a captured local, then
    :251 would clobber it and the live defect would be "swpctx is a silent no-op" -- a
    larger bug that the same one-token fix repairs.  Verified by opening the file: :165
    is `uint64_t a0 = cpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_A0]', a local captured at entry, so the fork
    does not exist.  The premise was true, and true by luck of my having read it earlier
    rather than by anything the brief showed.
    
    *** MY CENSUS WAS TEXTUALLY RIGHT AND MISLEADING, AND TWO SEATS FOUND IT
    SEPARATELY. ***  I reported EIGHT ALPHA_V0 return sites.  Three are inside `#if 0'
    and one is alpha_prom_call, so it is FIVE compiled, four live PAL returns -- codex
    and grok reached that independently.  I grepped for textual presence and reported it
    as compiled code.
    
    glm's triangulation point is the one worth keeping: the verdict holds EVEN IF both
    greps were wrong, because comment, code and third-party wrapper agree independently.
    The census was corroboration, never the load-bearing evidence, which is why an
    inflated count did not change the answer.
    
    SCOPE, deliberately narrow: the `// TODO: Don't invalidate EVERYTHING!' at :296 in
    the same arm is EXCLUDED -- every seat called it a separate row.  It bears on
    alphatbinegsel, which is open on the same flush behaviour, and since #514 landed
    first that row now owes a witness re-baselined with this fix present.  Queued for
    the flagship seat rather than answered here.
    
    Propagated to est, build, build-asan and /tmp/gxsec-build, all cmp-clean;
    build/ rebuilt and the two-part clearance re-run (binary 08:29:21 newer than source).
    The probe is registered CLASS_WITNESS, which section R accepts ungated.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
diff --git a/src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c b/src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c
index 7c66cd0..b511768 100644
--- a/src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c
+++ b/src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c
@@ -248,7 +248,18 @@ Make sure they are correct, as documented in the Manual.
 		    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_decrsv1);
 
 		/*  Return old context in v0.  */
-		cpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_A0] = cpu->cd.alpha.ctx;
+		/*  #514: was ALPHA_A0, contradicting the comment directly above and the
+		    third-party ABI wrapper.  `alpha_pal_swpctx' (alpha_cpu.h) declares
+		    `v0 __asm("$0")' as an OUTPUT and lists a0 as CLOBBERED, so a correct
+		    caller reads the result from v0 and may not rely on a0 -- the model was
+		    publishing into the register the caller is told to discard and leaving
+		    untouched the one it reads.  `:251' was the only `r[ALPHA_A0] =' in this
+		    file; every compiled return here uses ALPHA_V0.  No a0 write is added:
+		    the architecture marks a0 UNPREDICTABLE, which permits it to remain
+		    unchanged, so there is nothing further owed.  Ordering is unaffected --
+		    `a0' at the `ctx = a0' below is the LOCAL captured at function entry
+		    (`:165'), not a live register read, so this store cannot perturb it.  */
+		cpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_V0] = cpu->cd.alpha.ctx;	/*  #514  */
 
 		/*  Load new context:  */
 		{
