1460:
1461:            Both translation buffers (TBs) store recently used, instruction stream (Istream)
1462:            page-table entries and use a not-last-used replacement algorithm.
1463:
1464:            In addition, both ITBs support a register-enabled extension called the
1465:            superpage. The ITB superpage mappings provide one-to-one translation
1466:            from virtual program counter bits <33:13> to physical program counter bits
1467:            <33:13> when virtual address bits <42:41> = 2. When translating through the
1468:            superpage, the address space match (ASM) bit is always set in the PTE used in
1469:            the Icache. Access to the superpage mapping is allowed only while executing
1470:            in kernel mode.
1471:
1472:2-4 Internal Architecture
1473:            PALcode fills and maintains the ITBs. The operating system, through PALcode,
1474:            is responsible for ensuring that virtual addresses can be mapped only through
1475:            a single ITB entry (in the large page, small page, or superpage) at the same
1476:            time.
1477:
1478:            The IDU presents the 43-bit virtual program counter (VPC) to the ITB each
1479:            cycle while not executing in PALmode. If the PTE associated with the VPC is
1480:            cached in the ITB, the IDU uses the page frame number (PFN) and protection
1481:            bits for the page that contains the VPC to complete the address translation
1482:            and access checks.
1483:
1484:            The ITBs support a single address space number (ASN) through the PTE ASM
1485:            bit. Each PTE entry in the ITB contains an ASM bit. Write operations to
1486:            the ITBASM register invalidate all entries that do not have the ASM bit set.
1487:            This is a simple way to preserve entries that map operating system regions
1488:            while invalidating all other entries. (The ITBASM register is described in
1489:            Section 4.1.5.)
1490:
1491:            For more information about TBs and PTEs, see the Alpha Architecture
1492:            Reference Manual.
1493:
1494:2.2.3 Interrupt Logic
1495:
1496:            The IDU exception logic supports three sources of interrupts:
1497:
1498:            � Hardware interrupts
1499:
1500:                  - Three level-sensitive hardware interrupts are sourced by pins irq<2:0>.
1501:
1502:                  - Two internally generated interrupts respond to external interface error
1503:                       conditions. These are sourced by the memory controller error status
1504:                       register (ESR, Section 5.6.6) and the I/O controller status register 0
1505:                       (IOC_STAT0, Section 6.4.3).
1506:
1507:            � Software interrupts
1508:                  The 15 prioritized software interrupts are sourced by the software interrupt
1509:                  request register (SIRR, Section 4.1.13.2).
